    "title": "Special report: Spending on the rise in Virginia politics",
    "text": "Special report: Spending on the rise in Virginia politics\n(3) updates to this series since\nPolitical spending has grown by 90% to more than $312.6 million over the past three election cycles for statewide offices (governor, lieutenant governor and attorney general) and the House of Delegates.\nVirginia is one of six states that have no limits on what anyone or any company or organization can give a political candidate.\nSpending on lobbying is up 72% over the past three gubernatorial and House election cycles, a Richmond Times-Dispatch analysis of state Conflict of Interest and Ethics Commission data found.",
    "summary": "Special report: Spending on the rise in Virginia politics (3) updates to this series since Political spending has grown by 90% to more than $312.6 million over the past three election cycles for statewide offices (governor, lieutenant governor and attorney general) and the House of Delegates. Virginia is one of six states that have no limits on what anyone or any company or organization can give a political candidate. Spending on lobbying is up 72% over the past three gubernatorial and House election cycles, a Richmond Times-Dispatch analysis of state Conflict of Interest and Ethics Commission data found.",
